| Location | Average Daily Temperature | Attire |
| --- | --- | --- |
| New York City | 65°F | Light Jacket |
| Los Angeles | 75°F | T-Shirt |
| Miami | 85°F | Shorts and T-Shirt |
```
To visually represent the sun shining over each location, you can use an image of a sun or a sun icon. You can place the image next to each location in the table to indicate that it is sunny there. Here's an example of how you can add an image to the table using Markdown:
```
| Location | Average Daily Temperature | Attire | Weather |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| New York City | 65°F | Light Jacket | ![Sun icon](sun.png) |
| Los Angeles | 75°F | T-Shirt | ![Sun icon](sun.png) |
| Miami | 85°F | Shorts and T-Shirt | ![Sun icon](sun.png) |
```
In this example, the `![Sun icon](sun.png)` code adds an image of a sun to the table. You can replace `sun.png` with the name of the image file you want to use.
You can use Markdown to create the table and add the image, but you may need to use HTML to style the table and the image."}
 {'from': 'human', 'value': "That's very helpful! Can you give me some tips on how to style the table and image using HTML?"}
 {'from': 'gpt', 'value': "Sure! To style the table, you can use CSS. Here's an example of how you can create a CSS class for the table:
```

.table-style {
 border-collapse: collapse;
 width: 100%;
}
.table-style th, td {
 text-align: left;
 padding: 8px;
}
.table-style tr:nth-child(even){background-color: #f2f2f2}
.table-style th {
 background-color: #4CAF50;
 color: white;
}

```
In this example, the `border-collapse` property sets the table border to collapse, making it look cleaner. The `th` and `td` selectors set the text alignment and padding for the table cells. The `tr:nth-child(even)` selector sets the background color for every other row to make it easier to read. Finally, the `th` selector sets the background color and text color for the table header.

To style the image, you can use CSS as well. Here's an example of how you can create a CSS class for the sun icon:
```

.sun-icon {
 width: 20px;
 height: 20px;
}

```
In this example, the `width` and `height` properties set the size of the sun icon. You can adjust these values to make the sun icon bigger or smaller.

Once you have your CSS classes created, you can add them to your HTML code like this:
```
| Location | Average Daily Temperature | Attire | Weather |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| New York City | 65°F | Light Jacket | Sun icon |
| Los Angeles | 75°F | T-Shirt | Sun icon |
| Miami | 85°F | Shorts and T-Shirt | Sun icon |
```
In this example, the `class` attribute is added to the `table` and `img` tags to apply the CSS classes."}]

