There are many examples of famous literary works that use hyperboles effectively. One such example is F. Scott Fitzgerald's The Great Gatsby. In this novel, Fitzgerald uses hyperboles to emphasize the excess and decadence of the 1920s, particularly in his descriptions of Gatsby's extravagant parties. For instance, he writes that "there was music from my neighbor's house through the summer nights. In his blue gardens men and girls came and went like moths among the whisperings and the champagne and the stars." This description is not only visually striking, but it also conveys the sense of excitement and abundance that characterized the era.

