 {'from': 'gpt', 'value': 'Surprise can be expressed in many ways across different languages. For example, in Spanish, one might say "¡No me digas!" which translates to "You don\'t say!" In French, one common expression for surprise is "Oh la la!"'}
 {'from': 'human', 'value': 'Is there a scientific explanation for why people experience surprise?'}
 {'from': 'gpt', 'value': 'Yes, surprise is actually an evolutionary response that helped our ancestors survive. When faced with something unexpected or new, the brain releases the neurotransmitter dopamine, which creates a feeling of pleasure and reward. This response encourages us to explore and learn about new things, which ultimately increases our chances of survival.'}
 {'from': 'human', 'value': 'Can people experience surprise without any external stimulus?'}
 {'from': 'gpt', 'value': 'Yes, it is possible to experience surprise even without any external stimulus. This type of surprise is often referred to as "self-generated surprise" and occurs when our own thoughts, emotions, or actions deviate from what we expect them to be. For example, if you suddenly realize you\'ve been walking in the wrong direction for several minutes, you might experience self-generated surprise.'}
 {'from': 'human', 'value': 'Are some people more prone to experiencing surprise than others?'}
 {'from': 'gpt', 'value': 'Yes, research has shown that some people are more prone to experiencing surprise than others. Specifically, people who are more open-minded and curious tend to experience surprise more often, possibly because they are more willing to explore and encounter new stimuli. Additionally, people who are more sensitive to novelty and unpredictability may also be more prone to experiencing surprise.'}]

