[{'from': 'system', 'value': 'You are a helpful assistant, with no access to external functions.'}
 {'from': 'human', 'value': 'Please provide a comprehensive [ C++ code ] to display the various components of a computer system, including but not limited to the CPU, memory, storage devices, input/output devices, and other crucial components like the BIOS, Northbridge, Southbridge, cache memory, and power supply. It is crucial to include detailed comments in your code to explain the function of each component and their interactions with other elements of the system.'}
 {'from': 'gpt', 'value': 'Here is a C++ code that displays the various components of a computer system:
```
#include 
using namespace std;
int main() {
 // CPU
 cout << "CPU: Central Processing Unit" << endl;
 cout << "The CPU is the brain of the computer. It performs all the calculations and executes instructions." << endl;

 // Memory
 cout << "nMemory: RAM (Random Access Memory)" << endl;
 cout << "RAM is the temporary storage space used by the computer to store data and programs that are currently in use." << endl;

 // Storage Devices
 cout << "nStorage Devices: HDD (Hard Disk Drive), SSD (Solid State Drive)" << endl;
 cout << "HDD and SSD are used to store data and programs permanently. HDD stores data on spinning disks, while SSD uses flash memory." << endl;

 // Input/Output Devices
 cout << "nInput/Output Devices: Keyboard, Mouse, Monitor, Printer" << endl;
 cout << "Keyboard and mouse are used to input data into the computer. Monitor displays the output. Printer is used to print documents." << endl;

 // BIOS
 cout << "nBIOS: Basic Input/Output System" << endl;
 cout << "BIOS is a program that initializes and tests the hardware components of the computer during boot-up." << endl;

 // Northbridge
 cout << "nNorthbridge" << endl;
 cout << "Northbridge is a chip that connects the CPU to the memory and high-speed graphics cards." << endl;

 // Southbridge
 cout << "nSouthbridge" << endl;
 cout << "Southbridge is a chip that connects the CPU to the slower peripherals like USB, audio, and network cards." << endl;

 // Cache Memory
 cout << "nCache Memory" << endl;
 cout << "Cache memory is a small, high-speed memory that stores frequently used data and instructions." << endl;

 // Power Supply
